UID:296720@lms.astanait.edu.kz
SUMMARY:Final Exam is due
DESCRIPTION:FINAL EXAM [1] — INTEGRATED PROJECT PRESENTATION\n\nPrepare and
	 submit the final project presentation in PDF format.\n\nThe Final Exam [1]
	 demonstrates how the main parts of your project work together: the problem
	\, proposed solution\, user interface\, and database.\n\nTASK 1 — PROBLEM A
	ND TARGET USERS\n\nBriefly present the problem your project addresses.\n\nI
	nclude:\n\n	*\nWhat is the problem?\n\n	*\nWho are the target users?\n\n	*\
	nWhy is this problem relevant to these users?\n\n	*\nWhat difficulties do u
	sers currently experience?\n\nKeep this section short and focused on the fi
	nal version of your project.\n\n-------------------------\n\nTASK 2 — PROJE
	CT SOLUTION\n\nPresent the main idea of your project and explain how the pr
	oposed system helps solve the identified problem.\n\nInclude:\n\n	*\nWhat d
	oes the system do?\n\n	*\nWhat are its main functions?\n\n	*\nHow does the 
	proposed solution help the target users?\n\n	*\nWhat is the expected result
	 of using the system?\n\nFocus on the final version of the project\, includ
	ing the main features developed throughout the course.\n\n-----------------
	--------\n\nTASK 3 — HCI DIAGRAM AND USER INTERACTION\n\nPresent the final 
	HCI diagram / interface prototype of your system.\n\nShow the main screens 
	and demonstrate how the user moves through the system from the starting poi
	nt to the main outcome.\n\nThe presentation must clearly show:\n\n	*\nmain 
	screens\;\n\n	*\nmain buttons/actions\;\n\n	*\ntransitions between screens\
	;\n\n	*\nthe expected result of each important user action.\n\nThe HCI diag
	ram/prototype should represent the final version of the proposed system.\n\
	n-------------------------\n\nTASK 4 — HCI + DATABASE + SQL INTEGRATION\n\n
	Connect the main buttons/actions in your HCI prototype to the database.\n\n
	For each main functional button/action\, show:\n\nUser Action → SQL Query →
	 Database Result\n\nEach example must include:\n\n	*\nthe button/action fro
	m the interface\;\n\n	*\nthe corresponding SQL code\;\n\n	*\nthe database t
	able(s) involved\;\n\n	*\nthe result produced by the query\;\n\n	*\na short
	 explanation of what the user sees after the operation.\n\nFor example:\n\n
	Button: Find a Room\n\nSELECT *\n\nFROM room\n\nWHERE capacity >= 4\n\nAND 
	is_available = TRUE\;\n\nDatabase result:\nRooms 301\, 405 and 410 are retu
	rned.\n\nUser output:\nThe available rooms are displayed on the Search Resu
	lts screen.\n\nAnother example:\n\nButton: Confirm Booking\n\nINSERT INTO b
	ooking\n\n(student_id\, room_id\, booking_date\, start_time\, end_time\, st
	atus)\n\nVALUES\n\n(10234\, 405\, '2026-09-25'\, '15:00'\, '16:00'\, 'Confi
	rmed')\;\n\nDatabase result:\nA new booking record is created.\n\nUser outp
	ut:\nThe interface displays “Booking Confirmed.”\n\n-----------------------
	--\n\n \n\nTASK 5 — FUNCTIONAL CLICKABLE PROTOTYPE\n\nCreate a working\, in
	teractive prototype of your application.\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n\n	*\nThe protot
	ype must have a functional user interface.\n\n	*\nUsers must be able to nav
	igate between the main screens.\n\n	*\nButtons and other interactive elemen
	ts must perform meaningful actions.\n\n	*\nThe interface must follow the HC
	I principles studied during the course.\n\n	*\nThe prototype must represent
	 the main functionality of your project.\n\n	*\nThe prototype must be conne
	cted to your existing Supabase database.\n\nYou may use AI-assisted develop
	ment tools such as ChatGPT\, Cursor\, Lovable\, Bolt\, or other similar too
	ls to generate and improve your application.\n\nIMPORTANT: A static design\
	, screenshots\, or a presentation with clickable links is not considered a 
	functional prototype.\n\n-------------------------\n\n \n\nTASK 6 — SUPABAS
	E DATABASE AND SQL INTEGRATION\n\nConnect your interactive prototype to the
	 Supabase database created in Assignment 4 [2].\n\nDATABASE REQUIREMENTS:\n
	\n	*\nUse the existing database and tables from Assignment 4 [2].\n\n	*\nPr
	eserve the relationships between entities.\n\n	*\nConnect the application i
	nterface to the database.\n\n	*\nDemonstrate real database operations throu
	gh the interface.\n\nYour application must support the following SQL operat
	ions:\n\n		OPERATION\n		REQUIRED FUNCTIONALITY\n\n		SELECT with WHERE\n		Se
	arch for or filter specific records\n\n		Aggregation (COUNT\, AVG\, SUM\, o
	r GROUP BY)\n		Display calculated information\n\n		INSERT\n		Add a new reco
	rd through the interface\n\n		UPDATE\n		Modify an existing record\n\n		DELE
	TE\n		Remove an existing record\n\nFOR EACH OPERATION\, DEMONSTRATE:\n\n	*\
	nThe button or action in the interface.\n\n	*\nThe corresponding database o
	peration.\n\n	*\nThe result displayed to the user.\n\n	*\nThe actual change
	 in Supabase\, where applicable.\n\nYou may implement these operations thro
	ugh Supabase client methods\, APIs\, or SQL queries.\n\nEXAMPLE — SMART CAM
	PUS STUDY ROOM\n\nButton: Find a Room\n\nAction: Search for available rooms
	 with a capacity of at least four people.\n\nDatabase operation: SELECT wit
	h WHERE.\n\nUser output: A list of matching rooms appears on the screen.\n\
	nButton: Confirm Booking\n\nAction: Create a new booking record.\n\nDatabas
	e operation: INSERT.\n\nUser output: The interface displays "Booking Confir
	med"\, and a new record appears in Supabase.\n\nIMPORTANT: The application 
	must use the real Supabase database. Hardcoded results or simulated databas
	e operations are not accepted.\n\n-------------------------\n\n \n\nTASK 7 
	— FINAL DEMONSTRATION AND DEFENSE\n\nDuring the final defense\, demonstrate
	 your working application.\n\nYour demonstration must include:\n\nA short e
	xplanation of the problem and proposed solution.\nNavigation through the ma
	in screens.\nWorking buttons and interactive elements.\nReal database opera
	tions connected to Supabase.\nChanges in the database after user actions.\n
	An explanation of how AI tools were used during development.\n\nEvery team 
	member must understand the project and be able to explain how the interface
	 communicates with the database.\n\nSUBMISSION REQUIREMENTS\n\nSubmit a fin
	al presentation in PDF format containing:\n\nProblem and target users.\nPro
	ject solution and main functionality.\nScreenshots of the completed interac
	tive prototype with SQL queries.\nExplanation of the connection to Supabase
	.\nMain database operations.\nAI tools used during development.\nA link to 
	the working prototype.\n\nThe working prototype must be available for demon
	stration during the final defense.\n\nAI USAGE POLICY\n\nAI-generated code 
	is allowed. Students are not required to write every line of code manually.
	 However\, students are responsible for understanding\, testing\, and expla
	ining their application. A working application without an understanding of 
	its functionality is not sufficient for full points.\n\nTEAM SIZE\n\nSTANDA
	RD SCOPE\n\nREQUIRED SCOPE\n\n2 students\n\nStandard\n\nComplete problem an
	d solution overview\; final HCI diagram/prototype\; minimum 4 main function
	al buttons/actions connected to appropriate SQL queries and database result
	s.\n\n3 students\n\nExpanded\n\nComplete problem and solution overview\; ex
	panded final HCI diagram/prototype\; minimum 6 main functional buttons/acti
	ons connected to appropriate SQL queries and database results. The expanded
	 system must include functionality sufficient to justify the larger team si
	ze.\n\nDEFENSE PROCEDURE AND GRADING\n\n60% — SUBMITTED FINAL EXAM [1] PRES
	ENTATION\n\nEach student must upload the Final Exam [1] Presentation as one
	 PDF file before the deadline.\n\nThe presentation must contain all four re
	quired parts:\n\n	*\nProblem and Target Users\n\n	*\nProject Solution\n\n	*
	\nHCI Diagram and User Interaction\n\n	*\nHCI–Database–SQL Integration + pr
	ototype link\n\nThe presentation must follow the required structure and con
	tain all necessary diagrams\, interface elements\, SQL code\, database resu
	lts and explanations.\n\nThe presentation must be submitted in PDF format. 
	A file submitted in an incorrect format is treated as not submitted.\n\nAll
	 team members may submit the same group presentation\, but each student mus
	t upload the PDF individually.\n\nThe submitted presentation is worth a max
	imum of 60/100 points. The remaining 40 points can only be earned during th
	e scheduled Final Exam [1] defense.\n\n-------------------------\n\n40% — F
	INAL EXAM [1] DEFENSE\n\nThe team presents and demonstrates the final versi
	on of the project.\n\nThe defense must show the complete logical connection
	:\n\nProblem → Solution → User Interaction → User Action → SQL Query → Data
	base Result → User Output\n\nMaximum presentation time:\n\n	*\n2 students —
	 maximum 15 minutes\n\n	*\n3 students — maximum 20 minutes\n\nThe time limi
	t is strict.\n\nStudents are responsible for organizing the presentation so
	 that all required components are demonstrated within the allocated time.\n
	\nAny part that is not presented and explained before the time limit is rea
	ched receives 0 points for the corresponding Defense component of the rubri
	c.\n\nFor example\, if the HCI–Database–SQL Integration is correctly includ
	ed in the submitted PDF but the team does not reach or explain it during th
	e defense\, the team may receive the corresponding Submitted Presentation p
	oints\, but the corresponding Defense points are 0.\n\nThe instructor may s
	top the presentation immediately when the maximum time is reached. Addition
	al time is not provided for unfinished slides or explanations.\n\nStudents 
	should explain their work rather than read text directly from the presentat
	ion.\n\n-------------------------\n\nINDIVIDUAL ASSESSMENT\n\nAll team memb
	ers must participate in the Final Exam [1] defense.\n\nEach student must un
	derstand the complete project\, including the problem\, proposed solution\,
	 HCI design\, database structure and the connection between interface actio
	ns and SQL operations.\n\nAll team members may submit the same presentation
	\, but the Defense score is assessed individually. Therefore\, students fro
	m the same team may receive different Final Exam [1] grades.\n\nABSENCE FRO
	M DEFENSE\n\nA student who submits the required Final Exam [1] presentation
	 but does not attend the scheduled defense receives a maximum of 60/100\, p
	rovided that the other team member(s) successfully defend the project and e
	xplicitly identify the absent student's contribution.\n\nIf these condition
	s are not met\, submission alone does not guarantee 60/100.\n\nA student wh
	o does not attend the defense receives 0/40 Defense points.\n\n \n\nCRITERI
	ON\n\nTOTAL\n\nSUBMITTED PRESENTATION\n\nDEFENSE\n\nWHAT IS ASSESSED\n\nPro
	blem & Target Users\n\n15\n\n9\n\n6\n\nProblem and target users are clearly
	 identified\; relevance and user difficulties are explained.\n\nProject Sol
	ution\n\n15\n\n9\n\n6\n\nMain idea and functions of the proposed system are
	 clear and logically address the identified problem.\n\nHCI & User Interact
	ion\n\n25\n\n15\n\n10\n\nFinal HCI diagram/prototype is complete and logica
	l\; main screens\, actions\, transitions and outcomes are clearly presented
	.\n\nHCI–Database–SQL Integration\n\n45\n\n27\n\n18\n\nMain interface actio
	ns are correctly connected to SQL queries and database operations\; SQL cod
	e\, relevant tables\, database results and resulting user outputs are corre
	ctly demonstrated and explained.\n\nTOTAL\n\n100\n\n60\n\n40\n\n		 \n\n\n\n
	Links:\n------\n[1] https://lms.astanait.edu.kz/mod/assign/view.php?id=7692
	3\n[2] https://lms.astanait.edu.kz/mod/assign/view.php?id=81524\n
	
